 {:href "/venues/372"} "Taivallahti, Helsinki"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/255051"} "20 from 20 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/10762"} "Gert Fredriksson"] " of Sweden, the reigning Olympic champion in the " [:a {:href "/results/255024"} "K-1 1000"] " and " [:a {:href "/results/255030"} "10,000"] ", was the favorite to defend both of his titles at the 1952 Summer Olympics. He was also a multiple World Championship medalist from 1948 and 1950, with the most relevant for the Olympic K-1 1000 being his title in the event from 1950. Finland’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9560"} "Thorvald Strömberg"] ", meanwhile, was the current World runner-up."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Fredriksson came in third in his heat in the opening round, behind Strömberg and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10996"} "Lev Nikitin"] " of the Soviet Union, while the other heats were won by " [:a {:href "/athletes/9157"} "Rik Verbrugghe"] " of Belgium and " [:a {:href "/athletes/10371"} "Wim van der Kroft"] " of the Netherlands, the latter of whom was a bronze medalist from the " [:a {:href "/results/255007"} "1936 Olympic K-2 1000"] ". In the final, Fredriksson took command of the race at the halfway point and never relented, winning the gold medal and leaving silver for Strömberg, a reversal of their fortunes from the previous day’s " [:a {:href "/results/255058"} "K-1 10,000"] ". France’s " [:a {:href "/athletes/9632"} "Louis Gantois"] ", meanwhile, edged out van der Kroft for the bronze medal."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {} "R1"] [:th {} "Final"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "–"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/10762"} "Gert Fredriksson"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/SWE"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flag